   "subject": "how to get image matrix",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Dear Kyle,</div><div><br></div>how can I get an ion image without axes/labels, <span style=\"font-size:13px\">in the &quot;original&quot; pixel coordinates of the data ?</span><div>ie 1 ion image pixel = 1 image pixel, <span style=\"font-size:13px\">and pixel (x=1,y=1) has image coordinates (x=1, y=1)</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:13px\">or otherwise speaking, how to get the ion image as a simple 2-dim matrix of color values ?</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Thank you !</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><br></span></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "xE-7w1PAAgAJ",
   "from": "kbemis",
   "author": "6033d3c0e7d242fa",
   "date": "2016-04-26T01:08:27Z",
   "subject": "Re: how to get image matrix",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">The &#39;imageData&#39; slot of an MSImageSet object can be indexed as a datacube.<div><br></div><div>For example,</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">CardinalWorkflows</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">pig206</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>img1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> imageData</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">pig206</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,,]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># get 2-d matrix of first ion</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>img2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> imageData</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">pig206</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">features</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">pig206</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mz</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">888</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),,]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># get 2-d matrix of m/z 888</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><font color=\"#880000\"></font></div></code></div><br>These will give you the actual intensity matrix of the ion image, which you can then use or plot however you want.</div><div><br></div><div>To convert the intensities to colors, you could do, for example,</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">img3 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">as</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">raster</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">img2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> max</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">max</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">img2</span><font color=\"#666600\"><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> na</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span></font></font></div></code></div><br>to convert it to a raster object.</div><div><br></div><div>If you simply want to plot without axes/labels, without manipulating the intensity values, then use R&#39;s graphic parameters (see ?par).</div><div><br></div><div>For example, to plot an ion image without any axes or labels, you can do</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">par</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">bty</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;n&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> xaxt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;n&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> yaxt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;n&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>image</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">pig206</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mz</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">888</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> strip</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> colorkey</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> xlab</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ylab</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></font></div></code></div><br>Hope this helps!</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
